\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The same $500$ held-out problems, solved five other ways. Classical timings are one CPU core and one query; the learned rows are one GPU and $20$ samples per query, so the time column is not a like-for-like comparison. Classical clearance is averaged over solved problems; the learned rows are per-sample rates at $250$ training environments}}{7}{table.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:baselines}{{1}{7}{The same $500$ held-out problems, solved five other ways. Classical timings are one CPU core and one query; the learned rows are one GPU and $20$ samples per query, so the time column is not a like-for-like comparison. Classical clearance is averaged over solved problems; the learned rows are per-sample rates at $250$ training environments}{table.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Where the learned planner actually sits. The world-frame arm lies on the straight-line floor; the reduction lifts it by $30.1$ points; the classical planners remain far above. We show this rather than omit it: the contribution here is the representation result, not a competitive planner}}{7}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:baselines}{{1}{7}{Where the learned planner actually sits. The world-frame arm lies on the straight-line floor; the reduction lifts it by $30.1$ points; the classical planners remain far above. We show this rather than omit it: the contribution here is the representation result, not a competitive planner}{figure.1}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {B}Calibration: floors and ceilings}{7}{subsection.5.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:baselines}{{V-B}{7}{Calibration: floors and ceilings}{subsection.5.2}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The untrained stochastic baseline: Brownian bridges pinned at the endpoints, $20$ samples per query, same collision check. Widening the prior buys nothing at any setting, so best-of-$N$ over random detours is not what a learned sampler is competing against}}{8}{table.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:prior}{{2}{8}{The untrained stochastic baseline: Brownian bridges pinned at the endpoints, $20$ samples per query, same collision check. Widening the prior buys nothing at any setting, so best-of-$N$ over random detours is not what a learned sampler is competing against}{table.2}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Held-out performance against training-set size. ``+aug.'' is the world-frame arm trained with random $\mathrm  {SE}(3)$ motions of the whole problem. The world-frame arm gains $2.5$ points across a $12.5\times $ increase in environments and augmentation adds at most $2.1$ more, while the reduction gains $26.2$ and has not plateaued. Every world-frame and reduction cell is a mean over three seeds; the seed-matched differences are $+6.37\pm 0.19$, $+20.87\pm 1.56$, $+25.85\pm 0.37$ and $+30.08\pm 0.38$. The ``+aug.'' column is single seed, so it is not compared against a three-seed mean anywhere. The last column is the deployment metric, best-of-$20$ behind a collision check}}{8}{table.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:main}{{3}{8}{Held-out performance against training-set size. ``+aug.'' is the world-frame arm trained with random $\SE $ motions of the whole problem. The world-frame arm gains $2.5$ points across a $12.5\times $ increase in environments and augmentation adds at most $2.1$ more, while the reduction gains $26.2$ and has not plateaued. Every world-frame and reduction cell is a mean over three seeds; the seed-matched differences are $+6.37\pm 0.19$, $+20.87\pm 1.56$, $+25.85\pm 0.37$ and $+30.08\pm 0.38$. The ``+aug.'' column is single seed, so it is not compared against a three-seed mean anywhere. The last column is the deployment metric, best-of-$20$ behind a collision check}{table.3}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {C}The reduction scales; the baseline does not}{8}{subsection.5.3}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces $\mathrm  {SE}(3)$ taken apart one stage at a time, at $250$ training environments. Every bar is a measured arm rather than a term in a decomposition: the second is a translation-only reduction, trained and evaluated like the others. The increments between them are what each group of degrees of freedom is worth. The bottom bar adds all three mechanisms built for the residual $\mathrm  {SO}(2)$ --- roll augmentation, frame averaging and an equivariant backbone --- and is deliberately drawn on the same scale: its near-coincidence with the bar above is the paper's second finding. The dashed line is a straight segment from start to goal on the identical problems, which the world-frame arm does not reach.}}{9}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:cascade}{{2}{9}{$\SE $ taken apart one stage at a time, at $250$ training environments. Every bar is a measured arm rather than a term in a decomposition: the second is a translation-only reduction, trained and evaluated like the others. The increments between them are what each group of degrees of freedom is worth. The bottom bar adds all three mechanisms built for the residual $\mathrm {SO}(2)$ --- roll augmentation, frame averaging and an equivariant backbone --- and is deliberately drawn on the same scale: its near-coincidence with the bar above is the paper's second finding. The dashed line is a straight segment from start to goal on the identical problems, which the world-frame arm does not reach}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces The gap widens with data. If canonicalisation were compensating for a shortage of training data one would expect it to close; instead the world-frame baseline is nearly flat while the reduced model continues to improve. The hollow marker is the no-roll ablation, drawn on the treatment line because it is the same arm with one component removed}}{9}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:scaling}{{3}{9}{The gap widens with data. If canonicalisation were compensating for a shortage of training data one would expect it to close; instead the world-frame baseline is nearly flat while the reduced model continues to improve. The hollow marker is the no-roll ablation, drawn on the treatment line because it is the same arm with one component removed}{figure.3}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Decomposition by mechanism on the same held-out set, in percentage points against the arm without that mechanism --- so the rows are not additive, and the two indented rows decompose the row above them. Canonicalisation accounts for the effect at either budget, and both of its parts matter: recentring and axis alignment contribute $41\%$ and $59\%$. The two columns are the same mechanisms measured at $20$ epochs and at convergence; reading only the first would put frame averaging at zero, which Sec.\nobreakspace  {}\ref {sec:inert} shows is an artefact of the budget rather than a property of the mechanism. Dashes are comparisons we did not run to convergence. The $\mathrm  {SE}(3)$-augmentation and translation-only arms are single seed and are therefore differenced against the SAME seed, never against a three-seed mean}}{9}{table.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:ablation}{{4}{9}{Decomposition by mechanism on the same held-out set, in percentage points against the arm without that mechanism --- so the rows are not additive, and the two indented rows decompose the row above them. Canonicalisation accounts for the effect at either budget, and both of its parts matter: recentring and axis alignment contribute $41\%$ and $59\%$. The two columns are the same mechanisms measured at $20$ epochs and at convergence; reading only the first would put frame averaging at zero, which Sec.~\ref {sec:inert} shows is an artefact of the budget rather than a property of the mechanism. Dashes are comparisons we did not run to convergence. The $\SE $-augmentation and translation-only arms are single seed and are therefore differenced against the SAME seed, never against a three-seed mean}{table.4}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {D}Frame averaging's value scales with model quality; the roll ablation is unresolved}{9}{subsection.5.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:inert}{{V-D}{9}{Frame averaging's value scales with model quality; the roll ablation is unresolved}{subsection.5.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces (a) Canonicalisation is seven times the next largest mechanism. The band is $\pm 2$ across-seed standard errors, measured on the treatment arm at $60$ environments; frame averaging sits inside it, and the roll-augmentation bar rests on a single-seed ablation. (b) The non-equivariance residual of Eq.\nobreakspace  {}\eqref  {eq:resid} is flat across a $12.5\times $ range of training data --- it is not something the model acquires from seeing more layouts. The hollow marker shows that removing roll augmentation raises the residual by $64\%$ but leaves it under $3\%$}}{10}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:mechanisms}{{4}{10}{(a) Canonicalisation is seven times the next largest mechanism. The band is $\pm 2$ across-seed standard errors, measured on the treatment arm at $60$ environments; frame averaging sits inside it, and the roll-augmentation bar rests on a single-seed ablation. (b) The non-equivariance residual of Eq.~\eqref {eq:resid} is flat across a $12.5\times $ range of training data --- it is not something the model acquires from seeing more layouts. The hollow marker shows that removing roll augmentation raises the residual by $64\%$ but leaves it under $3\%$}{figure.4}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {E}What the residual does not predict}{11}{subsection.5.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:twosigns}{{V-E}{11}{What the residual does not predict}{subsection.5.5}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces The residual does not predict what a mechanism is worth. Augmentation and canonicalisation leave the learned field equally equivariant, and differ by $28$ points of held-out performance. Both columns are read from the SAME seed-0 checkpoints, since $r$ is a property of a particular trained field; the three-seed mean for the reduction row is $45.5$}}{11}{table.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:twosigns}{{5}{11}{The residual does not predict what a mechanism is worth. Augmentation and canonicalisation leave the learned field equally equivariant, and differ by $28$ points of held-out performance. Both columns are read from the SAME seed-0 checkpoints, since $r$ is a property of a particular trained field; the three-seed mean for the reduction row is $45.5$}{table.5}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {F}The third mechanism: a constrained architecture}{11}{subsection.5.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:arch}{{V-F}{11}{The third mechanism: a constrained architecture}{subsection.5.6}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces An $\mathrm  {SO}(2)$-equivariant backbone against the unconstrained one, $60$ environments, identical data and schedule. The constraint buys training efficiency and not a higher ceiling. The final row is seed-matched over two runs; the per-seed pairs are $50.9$ vs $50.8$ and $51.6$ vs $51.6$.}}{12}{table.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:arch}{{6}{12}{An $\mathrm {SO}(2)$-equivariant backbone against the unconstrained one, $60$ environments, identical data and schedule. The constraint buys training efficiency and not a higher ceiling. The final row is seed-matched over two runs; the per-seed pairs are $50.9$ vs $50.8$ and $51.6$ vs $51.6$}{table.6}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces The constraint buys optimisation, not accuracy. Both arms converge to the same held-out rate; the equivariant one arrives roughly four times sooner. Dotted lines mark where the unconstrained arm first reaches the equivariant arm's epoch-$10$ and epoch-$40$ values. Seed $0$ at $60$ environments; the seed-matched difference at epoch $300$ is $+0.15$ points.}}{12}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:convergence}{{5}{12}{The constraint buys optimisation, not accuracy. Both arms converge to the same held-out rate; the equivariant one arrives roughly four times sooner. Dotted lines mark where the unconstrained arm first reaches the equivariant arm's epoch-$10$ and epoch-$40$ values. Seed $0$ at $60$ environments; the seed-matched difference at epoch $300$ is $+0.15$ points}{figure.5}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Local geometry against the query frame, at convergence. Every cell is a mean over two or three seeds. Across-seed standard errors for the four cells of the main $2\times 2$ are $0.20$, $0.25$, $0.01$ and $0.10$: the local-geometry arms are the least seed-sensitive measurements in this work, with the world-frame arm reproducing to $0.01$ points across its seeds.}}{12}{table.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:localgeom}{{7}{12}{Local geometry against the query frame, at convergence. Every cell is a mean over two or three seeds. Across-seed standard errors for the four cells of the main $2\times 2$ are $0.20$, $0.25$, $0.01$ and $0.10$: the local-geometry arms are the least seed-sensitive measurements in this work, with the world-frame arm reproducing to $0.01$ points across its seeds}{table.7}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {G}Local geometry, and what the reduction is worth beside it}{12}{subsection.5.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:localgeom}{{V-G}{12}{Local geometry, and what the reduction is worth beside it}{subsection.5.7}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {H}How much of the level is training budget}{13}{subsection.5.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:budget}{{V-H}{13}{How much of the level is training budget}{subsection.5.8}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {I}Where the benefit comes from}{14}{subsection.5.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:mechanism}{{V-I}{14}{Where the benefit comes from}{subsection.5.9}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces What the scene code knows about the query. Ridge probes from the $128$-d obstacle embedding, and from the full conditioning vector, to the minimum clearance along the straight line from $\mathbf  {s}$ to $\mathbf  {g}$ --- the single thing a planner most needs to know about a query. Fitted and scored on \emph  {disjoint} environments, so a probe cannot memorise a scene; the conditioning row is the best score over $\lambda \in [1, 10^4]$}}{14}{table.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:probe}{{8}{14}{What the scene code knows about the query. Ridge probes from the $128$-d obstacle embedding, and from the full conditioning vector, to the minimum clearance along the straight line from $\svec $ to $\gvec $ --- the single thing a planner most needs to know about a query. Fitted and scored on \emph {disjoint} environments, so a probe cannot memorise a scene; the conditioning row is the best score over $\lambda \in [1, 10^4]$}{table.8}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Both arms evaluated across a $5\times $ range of clutter, on freshly generated layouts neither model has seen. Scoring needs no expert trajectories --- only obstacles, starts, goals and the collision checker --- so a density axis costs minutes rather than the days a training sweep would. Both arms are trained at $40$ obstacles, so every other column is out of distribution: this measures generalisation across density, not the effect of training at another one}}{15}{table.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:density}{{9}{15}{Both arms evaluated across a $5\times $ range of clutter, on freshly generated layouts neither model has seen. Scoring needs no expert trajectories --- only obstacles, starts, goals and the collision checker --- so a density axis costs minutes rather than the days a training sweep would. Both arms are trained at $40$ obstacles, so every other column is out of distribution: this measures generalisation across density, not the effect of training at another one}{table.9}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces The second state space, at two budgets an $8\times $ factor apart. The trivial baseline for this domain is geodesic interpolation --- straight in position, slerp in rotation --- the $\mathrm  {SE}(3)$ analogue of joining start to goal with a line. The world-frame arm gains $0.3$ points across the whole budget range and never clears its floor at any budget; the reduction clears it and is still climbing. On the deployment metric the world-frame arm moves \emph  {backwards}, as it does in the point-mass domain. Single seed per cell}}{15}{table.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:se3}{{10}{15}{The second state space, at two budgets an $8\times $ factor apart. The trivial baseline for this domain is geodesic interpolation --- straight in position, slerp in rotation --- the $\SE $ analogue of joining start to goal with a line. The world-frame arm gains $0.3$ points across the whole budget range and never clears its floor at any budget; the reduction clears it and is still climbing. On the deployment metric the world-frame arm moves \emph {backwards}, as it does in the point-mass domain. Single seed per cell}{table.10}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {J}The world-frame arm is the straight line at every density}{15}{subsection.5.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:density}{{V-J}{15}{The world-frame arm is the straight line at every density}{subsection.5.10}{}}
